/-!
# A transcribed presentation of the second Fischer group

This file carries the `Fi₂₃` row of the sporadic presentation data required by milestone S1 of
`TauCetiRoadmap/CFSGStatement/README.md`. It records presentation `GPLTable.Fi23.1` from Roderik
Lindenbergh's Group Presentations Library as a `TauCeti.GroupPresentation`, together with the exact
source, generator convention, transcription notes, expected counts, and decidable checks on the
transcription.

The presentation uses the Coxeter graph

```text
a --3-- b --3-- c --3-- d --3-- e --3-- f --3-- g --3-- j
                              |
                              3
                              |
                              h --3-- i
```

where all ten generators are involutions and every omitted edge has label `2`. The source encodes
this graph by the two paths `a3b3c3d3e3f3g3j,d3h3i`. It then appends

```text
(d c b d e f d h i)^10,  (a b c d e f h)^9.
```

Thus the graph contributes ten square relations, nine label-three relations, and thirty-six
omitted-edge relations. With the two appended words, the presentation has `57` relators and its
compiled words have `371` signed letters. The theorems below check those figures and cyclic
reduction from the transcribed data.

The library identifies this record as a presentation of `Fi₂₃` originating in Praeger--Soicher,
whose construction uses low-rank representations and graphs for sporadic groups. As an independent
transcription check, GAP 4.15.1 enumerates `31671` cosets of the subgroup generated by `a` through
`i`; the source identifies that subgroup as `2·Fi₂₂`, and `31671` is its index in `Fi₂₃`. This
computation is provenance rather than a Lean theorem: this file asserts no order, finiteness,
simplicity, or identification result. A separate source-to-Lean read-through remains part of the
S1 review artifact required by the roadmap.

The row is a sealed definition, so it publishes an equation for each of its fields: the transcribed
relator expressions with their generator indices written out, and the provenance a manifest row
exists to record. Together with `TauCeti.GroupPresentation.relators_def` and
`TauCeti.GroupPresentation.mem_relatorSet_iff` the first of those determines the compiled words and
the relations defining the presented group, so a consumer reasons about the row without unfolding
it.

## Main definitions and results

* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation`: the GPL finite presentation of the Fischer group `Fi₂₃`.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_transcribed` and the equations for the remaining fields: the
  characterization of the sealed row.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_map_length_relators`,
  `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_totalLength` and
  `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_relatorsCyclicallyReduced`: the three checks on the compiled
  words.

## References

* R. Lindenbergh, *Group Presentations Library*, version 1.0, record `GPLTable.Fi23.1`,
  <https://doris.tudelft.nl/~rlindenbergh/GPL/gpl.g>.
* C. E. Praeger and L. H. Soicher, *Low Rank Representations and Graphs for Sporadic Groups*,
  Cambridge University Press, 1997, Chapter 4, doi:10.1017/CBO9780511526039.005.
* The presentation-row characterization and audit theorem scaffold is adapted from the Janko-row
  formalization in <https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/pull/5283>.
-/

public section

